<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec_inductors" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  	<title>Inductors</title>

<!--		
    <video youtube="qMJK_sjdUCw"><description>DESCRIPTION</description></video>
-->
	
	<definition xml:id="def_inductor">
		<title>Inductor</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				
			</p>
			<p>An <term>inductor</term> is a circuit element that provides a voltage given by <me>V_{\text{inductor}} = -L \frac{dI}{dt}</me></p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<p>
						<alert>The sign of the voltage can be found using <xref ref="p_lenz"/>.</alert>  The induced voltage always opposes the change in flux through the inductor (this is denoted by the minus sign in the equation above).
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						<alert><m>L</m> is the inductance:</alert> a physical property measured in henries (H).  It depends on the size and shape of the inductor.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						<alert>Any circuit element can have an inductance.</alert>  Inductance tends to be most significant for wire coils and solenoids.
					</p>
				</li>
			</ul>
			<figure xml:id="x_fig_CD-L">
			<title>Inductor Circuit Diagram</title>
    		<caption>The circuit diagram representation for an inductor.</caption>
    		<image source="CD_L.png" width = "40%"><description>Left: a picture of a solenoid.  Right: a curling line representing an inductor in a circuit.</description></image>
		</figure>
		</statement>
	</definition>

	<definition xml:id="def_energy-inductor">
	<title>Potential Energy Stored in an Inductor</title>
	
	
		<statement>
			<p>
			</p>
			<p> The potential energy stored in an inductor is given by <me>U_{\text{inductor}} = \frac{1}{2} L I^2</me></p>
		</statement>
	</definition>
	
	
	<subsubsection>
		<title>Activities</title>
		<activity>
      <title>Sensemaking: Direction</title>
	  <introduction>
		<p></p>
			<p>Consider the inductor shown in the <xref ref="x_fig_CD-L"/> above.  Suppose at some instant in time the current is flowing downward and increasing in magnitude.  Use an area vector for the inductor that points upward.</p>
	  </introduction>
      <task>
	  <statement>
		<p>
			Use the right-hand rule to determine whether the current is <em>positive</em> or <em>negative</em> for the given area vector.
		</p>
	  </statement>
	  <answer>
		<p>
			Positive!
		</p>
	  </answer>
	  </task>
	  <task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Use the right-hand rule to determine whether the <alert>change</alert> in the current is <em>positive</em> or <em>negative</em> for the given area vector.
			</p>
		</statement>
		<answer>
			<p>
				The current is increasing, so the change is in the same direction as the current, which is positive!
			</p>
		</answer>
	  </task>
	  <task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Use <xref ref="p_lenz"/> and a <xref ref="rep_mag-induction-table-qual"/> to determine whether the induced voltage is <em>positive</em> or <em>negative</em>, and show that this matches the voltage equation in the definition of the <xref ref="def_inductor"/>.
			</p>
		</statement>
	  </task>
	  </activity>
	  <activity>
      <title>Inductor Behavior</title>
      <p></p>
			<p>Provide a physical argument to justify the generalized statement below about inductors.</p>
			<assumption xml:id="a_inductor-continuity">
		<title>Inductor Continuity</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				
			</p>
			<p>
				The current through an ideal inductor in a circuit should be continuous, but the voltage across an ideal inductor does not have to be continuous.
			</p>
			
		</statement>
	</assumption>
		</activity>
	</subsubsection>

  

	
<!--NO AI-->
</section>

